      "problem": "Let $a=49$ and $n=3.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n.$ Then, $q=$",
      "worked_solution": "Dividing $49$ by $3$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n16<49÷3<17.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=16.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by computing $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=49-(16)(3)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $49=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49={{16}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 3+{{1}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=16.$",
